  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Due to size and geometries of bases, the only possible pairing in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>DNA</mi></math> are between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></math> (guanine) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> (cytosine) through three <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>-bonds and between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> (adenine) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> (thymine) through two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>-bonds.</p><p>Thus, the complementary strand of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>DNA</mi></math> will be</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/f9d01d1b-61cb-4fd8-8254-611feaaced3e-image.png\" style=\"width: 641px; height: 116px;\" /></p>",
